          <!-- Section 9: YAML Manifests -->
          <h2 id="yaml-manifest-specifications">9. Production YAML Manifest Specifications</h2>
          <p>
            Examine production-ready, syntactically verified Kubernetes Service manifests for each of the three types:
          </p>

          <h3>A. ClusterIP Service Manifest</h3>
          <p>
            Notice that <code>spec.type</code> is set to <code>ClusterIP</code>. The <code>port: 80</code> defines the internal virtual IP listener, while <code>targetPort: 8080</code> directs traffic to the container application listener.
          </p>
          <CodeBlockWithCopy client:load code={clusterIPYaml} language="yaml" filename="clusterip-service.yaml" />

          <h3>B. NodePort Service Manifest</h3>
          <p>
            Here, <code>spec.type</code> is <code>NodePort</code>. Specifying <code>nodePort: 30080</code> is optional; if omitted, Kubernetes will automatically assign an available port between <code>30000</code> and <code>32767</code>.
          </p>
          <CodeBlockWithCopy client:load code={nodePortYaml} language="yaml" filename="nodeport-service.yaml" />

          <h3>C. LoadBalancer Service Manifest</h3>
          <p>
            The <code>spec.type</code> is <code>LoadBalancer</code>. In cloud environments like AWS EKS, annotations instruct the AWS Load Balancer Controller to provision an external Network Load Balancer (NLB).
          </p>
          <CodeBlockWithCopy client:load code={loadBalancerYaml} language="yaml" filename="loadbalancer-service.yaml" />

          <!-- Section 10: kubectl Inspection -->
          <h2 id="kubectl-inspection-commands">10. Practical Inspection Commands</h2>
          <p>
            When diagnosing Kubernetes networking in production, use these canonical <code>kubectl</code> commands:
          </p>

          <div class="space-y-4 my-6">
            <div class="p-4 rounded-lg bg-surface border border-border-subtle space-y-1.5">
              <div class="font-mono text-xs font-bold text-brand">kubectl get services (or kubectl get svc)</div>
              <p class="text-xs text-text-secondary font-technical">
                Lists all Services in the current namespace, showing their Type, ClusterIP, External IP, and mapped Ports.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="p-4 rounded-lg bg-surface border border-border-subtle space-y-1.5">
              <div class="font-mono text-xs font-bold text-brand">kubectl describe service &lt;service-name&gt;</div>
              <p class="text-xs text-text-secondary font-technical">
                Examines selector labels, active Endpoints, NodePort allocations, and events (e.g. cloud load balancer provisioning errors).
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="p-4 rounded-lg bg-surface border border-border-subtle space-y-1.5">
              <div class="font-mono text-xs font-bold text-brand">kubectl get endpointslices</div>
              <p class="text-xs text-text-secondary font-technical">
                <strong>Modern Kubernetes Endpoint API:</strong> EndpointSlices replace the older monolithic <code>Endpoints</code> resource to scale past 1,000 Pods per Service without bottlenecking the etcd control plane.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="p-4 rounded-lg bg-surface border border-border-subtle space-y-1.5">
              <div class="font-mono text-xs font-bold text-brand">kubectl get pods -o wide</div>
              <p class="text-xs text-text-secondary font-technical">
                Displays the internal Pod IPs and the specific worker node each Pod is currently scheduled upon.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>

          <!-- Section 11: Common Misconceptions -->
          <h2 id="troubleshooting-misconceptions">11. Common Misconceptions & Failure Scenarios</h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-6">
            <div class="p-5 rounded-lg border border-border-subtle bg-surface space-y-2">
              <div class="flex items-center gap-2 font-mono text-xs font-bold text-rose-500 uppercase">
                <AlertTriangle className="w-4 h-4" /> Misconception 1: "ClusterIP can be pinged from outside the cluster"
              </div>
              <p class="text-xs text-text-secondary font-technical leading-relaxed">
                <strong>Reality:</strong> A ClusterIP is a purely virtual IP managed via iptables/IPVS in the kernel of cluster nodes. It does not exist on external physical routers and does not respond to ICMP ping packets by default.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="p-5 rounded-lg border border-border-subtle bg-surface space-y-2">
              <div class="flex items-center gap-2 font-mono text-xs font-bold text-rose-500 uppercase">
                <AlertTriangle className="w-4 h-4" /> Misconception 2: "Service selector matches Deployment name"
              </div>
              <p class="text-xs text-text-secondary font-technical leading-relaxed">
                <strong>Reality:</strong> The Service <code>spec.selector</code> does NOT look at the Deployment resource name. It strictly matches the <code>labels</code> placed inside the <code>spec.template.metadata.labels</code> of the Pod specification. If labels do not match exactly, <code>Endpoints: &lt;none&gt;</code> will occur.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="p-5 rounded-lg border border-border-subtle bg-surface space-y-2">
              <div class="flex items-center gap-2 font-mono text-xs font-bold text-rose-500 uppercase">
                <AlertTriangle className="w-4 h-4" /> Misconception 3: "LoadBalancer works out-of-the-box on Bare Metal"
              </div>
              <p class="text-xs text-text-secondary font-technical leading-relaxed">
                <strong>Reality:</strong> In on-premise or local bare-metal clusters (e.g. kubeadm), a Service of type <code>LoadBalancer</code> will remain indefinitely in <code>&lt;pending&gt;</code> state unless an in-cluster load-balancer controller like <strong>MetalLB</strong> or <strong>Kube-VIP</strong> is installed.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="p-5 rounded-lg border border-border-subtle bg-surface space-y-2">
              <div class="flex items-center gap-2 font-mono text-xs font-bold text-rose-500 uppercase">
                <AlertTriangle className="w-4 h-4" /> Misconception 4: "Ingress is a 4th Kubernetes Service type"
              </div>
              <p class="text-xs text-text-secondary font-technical leading-relaxed">
                <strong>Reality:</strong> Ingress and Gateway API are NOT Service types. An Ingress is a separate Layer-7 reverse proxy (typically Nginx, Envoy, or Traefik) that routes HTTP hostnames and paths down to internal ClusterIP Services.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>

